## 4. Independent actual-figure mathematical and visual evidence

Figure ID: fig-choice-n33-exponentials-r1
Reviewer kind for later new_figure_reviews evidence: independent_agent
Reviewer identity: N33 math-independent-FIRST source/view reviewer, not the figure author
Verdict: PASS for this exact scratch candidate

### Written basis and source relationship

The allowed spec uses source.authoredBasis, not a fabricated original image.
Its authoritative text is the unchanged source 664: sketch 0.7^x and 0.4^x,
then use their graphs for equality and both strict inequalities.
The C/D names, line styles, unit grid, reference dots and continuation arrows
are declared authored instructional additions. No false graph alternative
or solution-set callout is drawn.

The reused independent source record already establishes the exact landmark
coordinates, decreasing positive exponential shape, asymptote, crossing and
relative order. Review compared the actual image against those saved facts,
not against an author choice key.

### Shared frame, formula/style mapping and visible curve order

At BOTH inspected sizes the image is nonblank and complete. There is one
shared Cartesian frame. Negative and positive x half-axes are present and
labelled -2,-1,0,1,2; the increasing-x arrow points right and the positive-y
arrow points up. The unit grid covers x=-2..2 and y=0..7, with 4 columns
and 7 rows. Both curves use that same grid and coordinate origin.

The visible legend pairs a solid line with "C: y = 0.7^x" and a dashed
line with "D: y = 0.4^x". The plotted C line is solid/dark and the plotted
D line is dashed/muted, matching both legend and RECOVERY1 stem. The caret
formula notation is unambiguous and legible. The styles remain distinguishable
without relying on hue.

On the negative x side, D is visibly above C: at x=-1, the D reference dot
is at y=2.5 and the C dot at y=10/7. On the positive side, C is above D:
at x=1, the C dot is y=0.7 and D is y=0.4. Both curves slope downward as
x increases, remain above the x-axis and flatten toward it to the right.
The displayed crossing is at x=0, y=1, not the origin (0,0) or (1,0).
The origin dot is separate and lies on the axes below the crossing.

### All visible reference dots and clipping

The actual rendered markers match the frozen coordinates:

| Marker | Required coordinate from frozen solve | Actual-image/geometry check |
| --- | --- | --- |
| origin | (0,0) | Visible axis-origin dot, separate from crossing |
| common point | (0,1) | Visible common curve dot aligned with x=0 and y=1 ticks |
| C at -2 | (-2,100/49) | Visible just above y=2, not moved to D's ordinate |
| C at -1 | (-1,10/7) | Visible between y=1 and y=2 |
| C at 1 | (1,0.7) | Visible above D's positive-side dot |
| C at 2 | (2,0.49) | Visible above the axis and D |
| D at -2 | (-2,6.25) | Visible between y=6 and y=7, fully inside frame/canvas |
| D at -1 | (-1,2.5) | Visible between y=2 and y=3 |
| D at 1 | (1,0.4) | Visible below C and above axis |
| D at 2 | (2,0.16) | Visible above the axis, not confused with y=0 |

The actual native D(-2) marker center is approximately
(125.902,191.716) px in the 960x1080 SVG. At 640 px width it is approximately
(83.934,127.811) px. Thus D=6.25 at x=-2 is not cropped at the top; its
full small marker footprint is inside the complete screenshot.
The highest D continuation arrow is also inside the top margin, and the
rightmost arrows and x label remain inside the right margin.

All four curve-continuation arrows are visible: C and D each point up/left
at their left displayed end and right/slightly down at the right end.
The rightmost D arrow is still above the x-axis, not an intersection.
Together with the two axis-direction arrows, all six directional arrows
are present and legible. They indicate continuation rather than closed
mathematical-domain endpoints. The question explicitly keeps x in all R.

### Independent rendered-geometry check, not just stored constants

An extra read-only browser query sampled each actual exported long SVG path
at 401 positions after its SVG transform, converted its screen coordinates
through the declared shared frame, and compared ordinates with its source
formula. The solid curve was classified by its actual undashed stroke and
the dashed curve by its actual dash array, not by a filename.

| Actual rendered curve | Visible sampled x range | Maximum sampled ordinate error against formula | Direction/range checks |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| C, solid | approximately -2.5..2.4 | 0.000003748 | x increases, y decreases, every sampled y>0 |
| D, dashed | approximately -2.2..2.4 | 0.000076598 | x increases, y decreases, every sampled y>0 |

These tiny interpolation/serialization errors are far below one screen
pixel at either reviewed size. They do not change landmark reading, curve
order or crossing. Queries at x=-2,-1,0,1,2 gave ordinates matching the
saved exact landmark values within 0.000001. In particular actual D(-2)
was approximately 6.249999729 and both paths at x=0 were approximately 1.

The actual visible marker centers were also returned by the browser:
common (0,1) and the eight curve landmarks align with their plotted paths.
Hidden JSXGraph construction points with zero-sized bounding boxes are not
counted as visible source dots or evidence of extra displayed points.
Unequal x/y physical scales are explicit in the spec, and both curves/axes
use the same transform; no curve-to-axis scale mismatch was observed.

### Normal-width text, completeness, culture and leakage

All 16 actual text nodes were measured through getScreenCTM():
native font size/minimum = 30 px at width 960;
normal rendered minimum = 20 px at width 640.
Thus every label exceeds the required 16 px at the declared normal width.
Every text bounding rectangle is fully inside the SVG at both sizes.
The full screenshots confirm readable formulas, axis names and tick values,
with no cropped rightmost legend/formula or bottom x ticks.

Visible wording is limited to C/D formulas, x/y and numeric ticks. It is
English/math notation, with no Cyrillic, cultural decoration or irrelevant
symbols. The legend lines and curves are visibly distinguishable in the
neutral rendering; no color-dependent key coding is present.

There are no equation or inequality solution sets, interval annotations,
marked correct option, stated left/right ordering rule, or textual (0,1)
answer callout in the figure. The crossing and heights can be read from
the plotted curves and ticks, which is the required problem data, not an
answer-bearing annotation. This is an instructional graph, not a solution
diagram whose text bypasses graph reading.

